<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New England</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> is the north-eastern region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> to Maine. It was settled from 1620 by English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> and other colonists who founded Plymouth, Massachusetts Bay, Connecticut, Rhode Island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>. Its merchants, fishermen and privateers traded across the Atlantic, and its colonies fought repeatedly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-england.jpg" alt="New England" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giacomo Barbaro · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portland_HeadLight_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fortress of Louisbourg on Île Royale, begun around 1720, guarded the Gulf of St Lawrence but fell to <b>New England</b> forces in 1745, before being returned in 1748.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…shaped the map: by the Treaty of Hartford of 1650 Stuyvesant provisionally ceded the Connecticut River region to <b>New England</b>, setting the eastern border 50 Dutch miles west of the river's mouth and just west of Oyster Bay on Long Island, a line the company refused to recognise…</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the North family farms, shipping and, from the 1790s, textile mills in <b>New England</b> dominated, while in the South plantations worked by enslaved people of African descent produced tobacco, rice and above all cotton, whose output soared after Eli Whitney's cotton gin of 1793; by 1860 nearly…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>One of them, the teenage ʻŌpūkahaʻia, known as Obookiah, reached <b>New England</b>, studied at the Foreign Mission School in Cornwall, Connecticut, inspired the mission to Hawaiʻi and told the missionaries about the language before they sailed in 1819.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…of limited government; persecuted groups such as the Huguenots and the English Puritans carried their faith abroad, the Puritans to <b>New England</b> from the 1620s; and the long experience of religious war strengthened the case for toleration that John Locke and others made later in the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Elsewhere Alexander Pushkin in Russia, Adam Mickiewicz in Lithuania and later in exile, Alessandro Manzoni in Milan with his novel The Betrothed, and Ralph Waldo Emerson and the Transcendentalists in <b>New England</b> gave the movement national forms.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li></ul></section>
